Local modifications
--------------------
Every local patch to the vendored `gllm/` tree MUST be appended below,
in order, so future contributors can tell upstream code from GreenBoost's
own changes at a glance.

- 2026-07-16: Initial import, no source modifications yet. Packaging is
  GreenBoost's own (`synapse_engine/pyproject.toml`, not upstream's
  setup.py) and `synapse_engine/logger.py` is a new stdlib shim (see below)
  — neither counts as a modification to the vendored `gllm/` tree itself.

- 2026-07-16: `gllm/layers/attention.py` — added a torch-SDPA fallback for
  the GENERAL (non-MLA, dense-model — Qwen/Llama/Mistral/etc.) attention
  path. Live bring-up on an RTX 5070 (Blackwell/sm120) found that
  `sgl_kernel.flash_attn.flash_attn_with_kvcache` (used unconditionally by
  `FlashAttention.forward()`/`_flash_attn_paged_varlen`) has NO hardware
  capability guard in this sgl-kernel build — unlike its sibling
  `flash_attn_varlen_func`, which correctly raises `NotImplementedError` via
  `is_fa3_supported()` — so it crashed the CUDA context with `no kernel
  image is available for execution on the device` instead of failing
  cleanly. This affected every dense model, not an edge case: the file's
  existing hardware-aware fallback (`MLAAttention._resolve_decode_backend`)
  is MLA-decode-only and never covers the dense path at all, and no triton
  (or any other non-FA3) kernel for dense paged-KV attention existed
  anywhere in the vendored tree to fall back to (the only triton kernel
  present, `triton_decode_attention.py`, is itself MLA-specific — its
  tensor shapes assume a compressed KV latent, not per-head K/V, so it
  cannot serve dense models regardless).
  New: `_sdpa_paged_varlen_fallback()` — gathers each sequence's KV out of
  the paged cache into a contiguous tensor and runs
  `torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention` per sequence (a Python
  loop over the batch, not vectorized — correctness-first for otherwise-
  unsupported hardware, not a performance path; sgl_kernel's flash_attn
  stays the fast path wherever `_FA3_AVAILABLE` is True). Builds an explicit
  bottom-right-aligned causal mask (SDPA's own `is_causal` is top-left-
  aligned and only correct when q_len == kv_len, which isn't true for
  prefill/decode mixed batches). `_flash_attn_paged_varlen` now checks
  `_FA3_AVAILABLE` (same signal `flash_attn_varlen_func` already uses) and
  routes to the fallback when false, with a one-time warning log.
  Live-verified end-to-end: `Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B` bf16, both streaming and
  non-streaming `/v1/chat/completions`, coherent output, HTTP 200 — with
  `--disable-cuda-graph` (CUDA graph capture separately OOMs on a 12 GB card
  with this model's 7.9 GB KV cache reservation plus graph-capture buffers;
  that is a distinct, not-yet-investigated finding, not caused by this
  patch, see workflow/gb-synapse.md).

- 2026-07-16: `gllm/async_utils.py` — `FutureMap.token_ids_buf` changed from
  `torch.empty` to `torch.zeros`. The buffer is a circular slot map of
  sampled token ids consumed by the next batch under overlap scheduling;
  `resolve_future()` can read a slot that `store_to_map()` hasn't written
  yet this early in a server's life (before the buffer has fully "warmed
  up" across enough requests to cycle every slot at least once). With
  `torch.empty` that read returned whatever bit pattern was already in that
  VRAM region, which as an int64 "token id" is essentially always outside
  the tokenizer's real vocab range. Confirmed live: this crashed the FIRST
  requests to a freshly started server (both a plain bf16 model and a GPTQ
  one, proving it is not quant-method-specific) with `OverflowError: out of
  range integral type conversion attempted` inside `tokenizer.decode`.
  `torch.zeros` makes an unwritten slot resolve to token id 0 (harmless,
  always valid) instead of undefined memory content. Live-verified as the
  actual, sufficient fix after re-testing in a genuinely clean process
  state (an earlier round had wrongly read this fix as "insufficient" —
  that read was contaminated by an orphaned gLLM worker process left over
  from a prior test iteration still holding GPU state; killed via
  `os.killpg`, see the `gb_synapse.py` `_kill_process_group` fix below,
  re-tested clean: sequential, 16-way concurrent, and long-generation runs
  against both bf16 Qwen3-0.6B and GPTQ TinyLlama all pass with zero
  crashes).

- 2026-07-16: `gllm/models/llama.py` — `LlamaAttention.__init__` never read
  `config.quantization_config` and never passed `quant_config=` to its
  `QKVParallelLinear`/`RowParallelLinear` sublayers, so `LlamaForCausalLM`
  (TinyLlama, Llama, Mistral-family) could never route through ANY
  quantized linear method regardless of the checkpoint's actual format —
  a pre-existing upstream gap, not something introduced by GreenBoost's own
  GPTQ additions below, but the thing that was blocking them from ever
  being reached for this architecture. Fixed by reading
  `getattr(config, "quantization_config", None)` and threading it through
  to both sublayers, matching the pattern already used elsewhere in the
  tree for other architectures.

