- 2026-07-27: `gllm/layers/rotary_embedding.py` —
  `RotaryEmbedding._compute_cos_sin_cache()` now computes on CPU and does one
  explicit `.to(device)` at the end, instead of creating tensors directly
  under whatever device context `__init__` runs in. Found live serving
  `Qwen/Qwen3-VL-8B-{Instruct,Thinking}-FP8` on a 12 GB card: the cache
  (sized to `max_position_embeddings` — hundreds of MB for this model's
  native 262144 context) is computed at the exact moment weights already
  occupy ~96% of VRAM, and `torch.arange`/`.cos()`/`.sin()`/`torch.cat`
  executed inside a `torch.utils._device` context hit a reproducible
  `RuntimeError: NVML_SUCCESS == ...nvmlDeviceGetHandleByPciBusId_v2_
  INTERNAL ASSERT FAILED` in this torch build (2.9.1+cu128) — confirmed
  independent of allocation size (reproduced at both the final `torch.cat`
  and the earlier `freqs.sin()` when a smaller ctx was requested) and
  independent of the GreenBoost shim (reproduces shimless too, there as an
  honest `CUDA out of memory` instead of the assert) — a genuine bug in this
  code path's interaction with `torch.utils._device`'s factory-function
  interception, not a transient resource issue. `.to(device)` is a tensor
  method with an explicit target, which does not route through that same
  interception (only argument-less factory calls like `torch.cat`/
  `torch.arange` do), so it sidesteps the broken path rather than working
  around its symptom. Matches upstream's own noted-but-skipped "more
  correct" approach (see the NOTE already in that function about HF
  implementation parity) — CPU computation costs nothing VRAM-wise, so this
  is a straight improvement, not a tradeoff.

