- 2026-07-28 (later the same day): `gllm/model_loader.py` — added
  `_skip_random_init()`, wrapping `load_model()`'s `model = model_type(self.
  config)` construction call. This is the final fix in the chain needed to
  actually serve Qwen3-VL-8B-{Instruct,Thinking}-FP8 (all prior entries in
  this file, plus the ones in `third_party/llama.cpp/NOTICE`'s sibling
  gllm/rotary_embedding.py, gllm/layers/attention.py, gllm/_custom_ops.py,
  gllm/layers/sampler.py entries, were prerequisites this one builds on).

  Root cause (isolated with a minimal 6-line repro, zero gllm code
  involved): constructing an `nn.Conv3d` under `torch.set_default_device
  ("cuda")` — exactly what `load_model()` does before building the model —
  triggers `reset_parameters()` -> `torch.nn.init.kaiming_uniform_` ->
  `Tensor.uniform_()`, a CUDA curand-based RNG-fill kernel launch. This
  crashes with `Fatal Python error: Floating-point exception` (SIGFPE)
  whenever the GreenBoost shim's LD_PRELOAD is active; the identical code
  works perfectly shimless. Confirmed via direct repro with and without the
  shim, same stack trace both failing runs, deterministic every time — a
  genuine bug in how the shim's `cuLaunchKernel` interception (heavily
  hooked for the cluster/feeder data-driven dispatch — scans kernel
  arguments for remote fake pointers) handles this particular kernel's
  argument layout. RNG kernels pass an internal generator/state struct
  unlike a typical elementwise-op kernel, which is the most likely point of
  difference the shim's argument scanner mishandles — NOT root-caused at
  the shim/C level yet, only isolated and worked around at the gllm level.

  The fix does not touch the shim: the random values `kaiming_uniform_`
  writes here are immediately discarded anyway — every parameter gets
  overwritten by real checkpoint weights in `model.load_weights()` a few
  lines later in the same function. `_skip_random_init()` monkey-patches
  `torch.nn.init`'s RNG-based functions (`kaiming_uniform_`,
  `kaiming_normal_`, `xavier_uniform_`, `xavier_normal_`, `normal_`,
  `uniform_`, `trunc_normal_`) to no-ops for the scope of model
  construction only, restoring the originals immediately after — the same
  standard practice HuggingFace `transformers` uses in its own
  `no_init_weights()` context manager to skip wasted random initialization
  when loading a pretrained checkpoint. Verified this patches correctly
  (module-attribute lookup at call time, not a pre-bound import — confirmed
  by reading `torch/nn/modules/conv.py`'s own `from torch.nn import ...
  init` and `init.kaiming_uniform_(...)` call site) and verified the crash
  is avoided with a standalone repro before deploying to the real
  `model_loader.py`.

  **Follow-up not done**: the actual shim bug (whatever `cuLaunchKernel`
  does wrong for curand-style kernels) is still open — this workaround
  means gLLM never hits it during model construction, but any OTHER code
  path that does RNG-based tensor fills directly under the shim (outside
  `torch.nn.init`, or outside model construction) could still hit the same
  SIGFPE. Worth root-causing at the shim level eventually, tracked as part
  of the standing T1/T2/shim-ownership direction in
  `workflow/known-issues.md`, not blocking anything today.

