  `hidden_states` (cuda) to `deepstack_input_embeds[...]` (cpu).
  Root-caused to `qwen3_vl.py:540`: `self.deepstack_input_embeds =
  [torch.zeros(...), ...]` — a PLAIN Python list of tensors (despite the
  adjacent "register buffer for deepstack" comment; it never actually calls
  `register_buffer()`). `model.to("cuda")` only walks `nn.Module`'s own
  `_parameters`/`_buffers`/`_modules` bookkeeping, so this list — allocated
  under the scoped `torch.device("cpu")` construction context along with
  everything else — never gets moved, and stays stranded on CPU forever.
  Fixed generally, not by special-casing this one attribute: added
  `_move_stray_cpu_tensors()`, called right after `model.to("cuda")` in
  `load_model()`, which walks every submodule's plain (non-underscore)
  instance attributes and moves any CPU tensor or list/tuple of CPU tensors
  it finds. This closes the whole "model class forgot to actually call
  register_buffer" shape of bug, not just Qwen3-VL's deepstack path — the
  CPU-construct architecture in this file makes ANY such attribute across
  the whole vendored model zoo susceptible, the same "unbounded whack-a-
  mole" reasoning that motivated CPU-construct itself now applies one level
  up to this attribute-tracking gap.

- 2026-07-28 (perf investigation, NOT a correctness bug — recorded so the
  next person doesn't re-walk the same dead ends): Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct-FP8
  serves and generates correctly (see the sixth-crash entry above) but very
  slowly — ~4-7s/token measured live. Investigated two candidate fixes,
  both real dead ends, confirmed by measurement not assumption:
  (a) `flash-attn-4` (CuTeDSL, has genuine dedicated sm_120 kernel files
  — `flash_fwd_sm120.py`/`flash_bwd_sm120.py`, confirmed by inspecting the
  installed package, not marketing copy) was pip-installed and benchmarked
  directly against this GPU. Prefill-shaped (2048-seq self-attention):
  0.92x vs torch SDPA (FA4 SLOWER). Decode-shaped (1 query token against a
  2000-token KV cache — the actual bottleneck's shape): 0.46x vs SDPA (FA4
  2.2x SLOWER). torch's built-in SDPA already dispatches to a solid backend
  on this GPU for both shapes; FA4's sm_120 kernel is not competitive here.
  More importantly: at ~0.05-0.1ms per attention call either way, attention
  is nowhere near the ~4000ms/token bottleneck — swapping attention kernels
  cannot fix this regardless of which one wins the microbenchmark. Reverted
  the install (it also dragged in conflicting nvidia-cutlass-dsl 4.6.0.dev0
  and protobuf 6.33.6, breaking this package's own ==4.5.0 pin and
  gptqmodel's >=7.34.0 — restored both). One operational gotcha from the
  revert worth remembering: `pip uninstall flash-attn-4` left an EMPTY
  `flash_attn/` namespace-package directory behind in site-packages, which
  made `transformers.utils.import_utils.is_flash_attn_2_available()` crash
  with `KeyError: 'flash_attn'` on every subsequent launch (spec found via
  the residual directory, but no matching installed-distribution metadata
  in PACKAGE_DISTRIBUTION_MAPPING) — pip's uninstaller doesn't always clean
  up empty namespace-package dirs; had to `rmdir` it manually.
  (b) CUDA graph capture (`GB_SYNAPSE_TORCH_CUDA_GRAPH=1` /
  `gb_synapse.serve(cuda_graph=True)`, see gb_synapse_backends.py) was
  already off by default for a documented reason (2026-07-16: OOM on this
  12GB card). Retested now that ctx=8192 (down from the 65536 default)
  leaves real headroom, in case the OOM was ctx-specific. It is NOT
  ctx-specific: capture crashes immediately at "Capturing CUDA Graphs: 0%"
  with `CUDA error: an illegal memory access was encountered` (from
  `createEvent`, `c10/cuda/CUDAEvent.h:261`), a fatal C-level abort, not an
  OOM. This is a DIFFERENT, more fundamental incompatibility than the one
  already on record — CUDA graph capture and this GreenBoost shim build
  (LD_PRELOAD CUDA/NVML interception) do not coexist AT ALL on this box,
  independent of available VRAM. Do not re-attempt cuda_graph=True here
  without first root-causing the shim's interaction with CUDA event/stream
  capture APIs specifically (a different code path than the ambient-
  default-device kernel-launch bug documented above, so likely a distinct
  root cause, not the same one recurring).
  RESOLVED, same day, by direct measurement — the "~4-7s/token" framing
  above was itself wrong, not just the two ruled-out fixes. `py-spy dump
  --native` sampling (15 samples at 1Hz, sudo, against the live worker PID
  during a real vision request) caught the worker sitting IDLE in
  `recv_ipc_package`/zmq poll the large majority of the time, with every
  "active" sample in scheduler bookkeeping (`check_abort_seqs`,
  `_build_prefetched_input`, `schedule_once`) — never once inside a vision-
  tower or attention forward pass, which would be impossible if those ops
  genuinely cost seconds each. That contradiction prompted re-timing
  fresh: the ORIGINAL 152s/38-token measurement was the FIRST-EVER
  inference call on a just-loaded server (Triton/cuBLAS kernel autotuning
  for never-before-seen shapes pays a real one-time cost). Two more vision
  requests on the SAME already-warm server, timed directly: 9.3s/31 tokens
  and 11.3s/40 tokens — both ≈0.28s/token (~3.6 tok/s), consistent with
  each other and with the earlier text-only measurement (26.4s/100 tokens
  ≈ 0.26s/token). Steady-state throughput was never the problem; the
  original number was a cold-start artifact mistaken for a sustained rate.
  ~3.6 tok/s for an 8B FP8 model on a 12GB consumer card via the SDPA
  fallback (no flash-attn, no CUDA graphs) is reasonable, not a regression
  to chase further. Neither the flash-attn-4 revert nor the CUDA-graph
  finding above needed to happen for this model to be usable — both are
  still true and still worth keeping on record (real, reproducible
  findings), just not the fix this investigation was looking for.

