  `vllm/model_executor/layers/mamba/ops/` (Apache-2.0, itself adapted from
  `https://github.com/state-spaces/mamba/blob/v2.2.4/mamba_ssm/ops/triton/`),
  not hand-written -- a subtly-wrong SSD scan produces silently-corrupt
  output, not a crash, so reusing Tri Dao/Albert Gu's own battle-tested
  Triton source (the same reuse-validated-kernel pattern already used for
  GPTQ/AWQ dequant via the vendored `gptqmodel` Triton kernel) was the
  deliberate choice over writing new numerically-sensitive kernel math
  under time pressure. Zero new pip dependencies -- `einops`/`packaging`
  were already present. Two deliberate deviations from upstream, both
  GPU-only-appropriate for this codebase:
    1. `ssd_combined.py`: the trailing `current_platform.is_cpu()` override
       block (swaps in a CPU-only kernel) is dropped entirely --
       GreenBoost's Immutable Design Rule forbids CPU tensor compute
       outright, so the Triton-only path is always used, unconditionally.
    2. `mamba2_decode.py`: `selective_scan_fn` (the older Mamba-1 API) and
       its CPU/XPU fallback branches are dropped -- Mamba-2's decode only
       needs `selective_state_update`, and `selective_scan_fn` pulled in a
       compiled-CUDA-extension dependency (`vllm._custom_ops.
       selective_scan_fwd`) with no reason to build here. Also simplified:
       the benchmark-tuned per-GPU JSON config lookup for the decode
       kernel's launch config is replaced by calling the hard-coded
       `_get_default_ssm_launch_config` heuristic directly -- upstream
       itself falls back to exactly this heuristic whenever no config file
       is found, which is the *only* outcome possible here since the
       tuned-config JSON tree was never vendored (a real, documented
       perf-tuning gap, not a correctness one).
  `causal_conv1d_triton.py` (already vendored for GDN) is reused as-is --
  GDN and Mamba-2 use the identical short causal depthwise convolution.

  A chunk-boundary metadata helper, `_compute_chunk_metadata()` in
  `mamba.py` itself (plain Python, not vendored), derives
  `cu_chunk_seqlens`/`last_chunk_indices`/`seq_idx` from the packed varlen
  `query_start_loc` gLLM already computes for FlashAttention/GDN -- vLLM
  precomputes the equivalent in its own attention-metadata builder, a
  scheduler-integrated component gLLM has no equivalent of for the SSD
  chunk boundary specifically.

  **Two real correctness bugs found via a live next-token divergence
  investigation** (gLLM predicted " assertEquals"/garbage-repetition for
  "The capital of France is" against `AntonV/mamba2-130m-hf`; HF's own
  reference predicted the sane " a"/" in"/" the") — isolated by a direct
  tensor-level comparison against `transformers/models/mamba2/
  modeling_mamba2.py` (fetched and read directly, not assumed), calling
  the vendored kernels with HF's own weights to prove the KERNELS were
  correct before concluding the bug was in the model-file glue code:
    1. `Mamba2Mixer.norm` was constructed with `norm_before_gate=True,
       group_size=self.head_dim` — copied verbatim from
       `Qwen3_5GatedDeltaNet`'s gated-norm convention without re-deriving
       it for Mamba-2. HF's real `MambaRMSNormGated.forward()` computes
       `norm(x * silu(gate))` (gate applied BEFORE norm, variance over the
       FULL width) — the OPPOSITE order, and NOT grouped. Fixed:
       `group_size=None, norm_before_gate=False`.
    2. `_rmsnorm_for()` (the plain decoder-level pre-mixer/final norm) used
       `gllm.layers.layernorm.GemmaRMSNorm` purely for its convenient
       `(x)->x`/`(x,residual)->(x,residual)` call signature, without
       checking its NORMALIZATION MATH: `GemmaRMSNorm` interprets its
       stored weight as `(weight + 1)` at runtime (correct for Qwen3.5,
       trained Gemma-style) — wrong for Mamba-2's real checkpoints (plain
       `weight * normalize(x)`, confirmed against the HF source). Fixed:
       swapped to `gllm.layers.layernorm.RMSNorm` (same file, identical
       call signature, standard math).
  Live-verified after both fixes: a direct tensor comparison of layer 0's
  mixer output against HF's own `mixer_ref()` call on identical input
  (weights copied verbatim, bypassing gLLM's own weight loader) matched to
  `mean abs diff: 0.0046` (bf16 rounding noise). End-to-end: real
  `/v1/completions` requests through the live gb-synapse proxy now produce
  coherent English ("a city of the French people, and the capital of the
  French Republic...") at ~77 tok/s on an RTX 5070, confirmed via
  `nvidia-smi` polling to be genuine GPU compute (not a silent CPU
  fallback — a hypothesis raised and disproven with concrete evidence: the
  "Triton is not supported on current platform, roll back to CPU"
  warning seen in logs is real but gates only unrelated tuning flags
  (TF32, Hopper-specific paths) in `gllm/layers/ops/fla/utils.py`, not
  actual compute placement — verified by reading every kernel file's own
  CPU-branch gating, all of which are either absent (this vendored SSD/
  decode/conv code) or dead-code (`layernorm_gated.py`'s `_use_cpu`, which
  requires `cpu_has_amx_support()`, a hardcoded-`False` stub).

  **Four orthogonal, pre-existing gLLM engine bugs found and fixed along
  the way** — none specific to Mamba-2's math, all hit because this was
  the first-ever `num_kv_layers=0` (truly zero real KV cache, not just a
  hybrid with fewer KV layers) model to run through this engine:
    - `model_loader.py:load_config()`: a bare `assert hasattr(self.config,
      "text_config")` crashed on checkpoints with neither `dtype` nor
      `text_config` (confirmed live: `AntonV/mamba2-130m-hf`'s config.json
      has neither). Now falls back to `torch.bfloat16` with a logged
      warning instead of asserting.
    - `memory_manager.py`: `MemoryManager.init()` divided by
      `get_sizeof_KV_per_page()` (legitimately 0 when `num_layers=0`) —
      `ZeroDivisionError` on every load. `Segment.get_memory_util()` and
      `MemoryManager.get_memory_free()` had the same unguarded-zero-
      denominator shape for their own scheduler-logging divisions.
      `pre_allocate_page()` (both the base and `PrefixMemoryManager`
      overrides) now no-ops when `num_pages == 0` instead of calling
      `segment.allocate()` on an empty pool.
    - `scheduler.py` (`chunked_prefill`/`schedule_prefill_batch`/
      `check_preempt`): three page-budget admission checks unconditionally
      computed `page_size * max(free_pages - reserve_pages, 0)`-shaped
      expressions that always evaluate to zero when there is no page pool
      at all, permanently starving admission — an infinite silent hang
      (`#wait` stuck at 1, `#run` stuck at 0), not a crash. All three now
      skip the page-budget clamp when `self.memory_manager.num_pages == 0`
      and rely on the SSM working-slot check (already present, already
      correct) as the real admission gate for a pure-recurrent model.
    - `input_data.py::_cal_slot_mapping()`: indexed into every seq's
      (correctly empty) `page_table` unconditionally —
      `IndexError: index 0 is out of bounds for axis 0 with size 0`. Now
      zero-fills and returns early when there's no real KV cache (the
      slot mapping is never consumed by anything for a model with no
      FlashAttention layer). The zero-page check here uses
      `getattr(self.memory_manager, "num_pages", 0)` rather than a direct
      attribute access — matching this same file's own existing
      `getattr(self.memory_manager, "segment", None)` pattern a few lines
      up — because this code runs in the overlap-worker subprocess, where
      `num_pages` was observed not yet present as a plain attribute at the
      point this runs (a cross-process initialization-order detail not
      fully root-caused; the defensive default is safe and sufficient
      regardless of the exact cause).

